    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is coherent, additive, and consistent with the patterns this codebase already establishes.\n\n- `compose()` is placed in `OwnerAddress` alongside `normalize()`, keeping the address-formatting rule as a single source of truth and mirroring the existing \"one utility class per concern\" idiom (`OwnerTelephone`, `OwnerEmail`, `OwnerHousehold`). Natural home.\n- The resolution logic (prefer structured, fall back to flat, else error) lives in `ValidateOwnerFields`, which is exactly where the existing validate-and-canonicalize pattern already sits for telephone (\u2192 E.164) and email (\u2192 lowercase). The new address branch follows that same shape, so it reads as more of the same rather than a new concern bolted on.\n- Writing the resolved value back into the flat `address` field is a clean choice: it concentrates the structured-vs-flat fallback in one place instead of scattering `getAddressLine1() != null ? \u2026 : getAddress()` across every downstream reader (`ApplyOwner`/mapper, household, postcode). Those readers stay unchanged.\n- Entity fields, the four DB schemas, and the OpenAPI schema are all updated consistently, and the DTO\u2192entity mapping is left to the existing MapStruct name-based mapper, so the structured fields flow through without hand-written plumbing.\n\nOne thing I looked at and concluded is *not* worth changing: in the structured branch the caller normalizes `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` and then `compose()` normalizes them again. It's redundant work, but `normalize` is idempotent and `compose`'s self-contained \"normalize then join\" contract is the safer, more reusable design; removing the redundancy would either weaken that contract or require `compose` to also hand back the per-line values, which is more complexity than it saves. Not a structural defect.\n\n(Note, outside this diff's scope: `ApplyOwner` \u2014 the update path \u2014 copies only `address` and not the new `addressLine1/2`, so the entity's structured columns could drift from `address` on update. The spec and this change only cover create, so it's not a fault of the diff, but it's the natural next place this feature will need attention.)",
